<!-- Minimal Arcade version file for the 15.9 servicing line.

     15.9 does NOT control product or dependency versions from here. Package versions live in
     scripts/build/TestPlatform.Dependencies.props and the product version in
     scripts/build/TestPlatform.Settings.targets, exactly as they always have. This file exists
     only because the Arcade SDK requires it. Do not grow it into a second source of truth.

     MEASURED 2026-09-02, AND THIS FILE IS ALREADY A SECOND SOURCE OF TRUTH. VersionPrefix below
     says 15.9.3; scripts/build/TestPlatform.Settings.targets says TPVersionPrefix 15.9.2 and
     appends -dev. Both are live, and a single build stamps BOTH strings:

       artifacts\bin\datacollector\Release\net451\win7-x64\...CoreUtilities.dll   15.9.2-dev
       artifacts\bin\datacollector\Release\netcoreapp1.0\...CoreUtilities.dll     15.9.3-ci

     scripts/build.ps1 -s Publish republishes most components and stamps 15.9.2-dev from the
     settings file. It does not republish Microsoft.TestPlatform.Extensions.BlameDataCollector or
     ...EventLogCollector, so those keep Arcade's 15.9.3-ci. The delivered VSIX therefore carries
     172 files at 15.9.2-dev and 28 at 15.9.3-ci. All 200 carry FileVersion 42.42.42.42424, which
     is Arcade's placeholder, so ALL of them are compiled from this branch - nothing is restored,
     stale, or missing, and a servicing change WILL reach the artifact.

     The shipped 15.9.1 VSIX carries ONE version across all 200 files. This is a regression
     introduced by placing Arcade beside the 2018 build.

     FIXING IT IS A DECISION, NOT A CLEANUP: reconciling the two makes the line claim to be either
     15.9.2 or 15.9.3, which is a servicing question. Whoever takes it should change ONE of these
     two files and have the other read it - the two files are the finding, not the symptom.

     RESOLVED 2026-09-02: use 15.9.3 in both places.
     scripts/build/TestPlatform.Settings.targets TPVersionPrefix moved 15.9.2 -> 15.9.3. The VERSION
     VALUES in this file were NOT changed; it is the source that was standardised on. The measurement
     above is kept as written because it is what motivated the ruling, and it describes the state
     BEFORE the change.

     PRECISION ABOUT "NOT TOUCHED", because the looser wording was here first and was wrong. This
     file HAS been modified - by these very appends. Verified against 57864a268^: of the 18 lines of
     the pre-ruling measurement, 17 are byte-identical and the eighteenth is the line that CLOSED the
     comment, which moved down because the appended text goes inside the same comment block.

     That is a structural limit rather than an oversight, and it is worth naming: appending inside an
     XML comment CANNOT be byte-append, because the closing marker has to move. 16.11's equivalent
     record is Markdown, where append-only is literal and they proved all 408 original lines
     byte-identical. The same guarantee is not available in this file, so claiming it here would have
     been false. What IS true, and is what the guarantee is for: no line of the measurement was
     edited to agree with the outcome that arrived after it.

     AND WRITING THIS PARAGRAPH BROKE THE FILE ONCE. The first draft quoted the closing marker
     literally, inside the comment, which ended the comment there and left the rest as stray text -
     the build failed immediately. An XML comment cannot contain a double hyphen at all, so a comment
     explaining comment terminators is the one place that sequence cannot be written. Same shape as a
     grep for a fixed property matching the note that says it was fixed: the documentation of a
     construct is where the construct is least able to appear.

     THE RULING RECONCILES THE PREFIX, NOT THE SUFFIX. The artifact still carries two
     ProductVersion strings. Since 2026-09-02 the majority one is release-<yyMMdd>-<rev>, computed
     per build in azure-pipelines-official.yml, and the minority 28 still carry Arcade's -ci. The
     28/172 split is unchanged and still unfixed: it is decided by which components
     scripts/build.ps1 -s Publish regenerates, which is upstream of both the VSIX and the
     packages. -->

<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <VersionPrefix>15.9.3</VersionPrefix>
    <PreReleaseVersionLabel>hotfix</PreReleaseVersionLabel>
    <PreReleaseVersionIteration></PreReleaseVersionIteration>
    <!-- AssemblyVersion is pinned at 15.0.0 because the wire protocol serializes against it. -->
    <AssemblyVersion>15.0.0</AssemblyVersion>
    <UsingToolNetFrameworkReferenceAssemblies>true</UsingToolNetFrameworkReferenceAssemblies>
    <!-- 15.9 pins its own xunit / MSTest / NUnit versions in scripts/build/TestPlatform.Dependencies.props.
         Arcade's defaults are far newer and are not compatible with netcoreapp1.0.

         Select the test runner explicitly instead of setting the deprecated UsingToolXUnit=false.
         Both keep Arcade's xunit packages away, because Arcade imports
         tools\$(TestRunnerName)\$(TestRunnerName).targets and only the XUnit one adds those
         references - but UsingToolXUnit=false does it by blanking TestRunnerName entirely, and
         RunTests is defined inside the runner targets file. So blanking the name also removes the
         target, and every test project then fails with
           Tests.targets(30,23): error MSB4057: The target "RunTests" does not exist in the project.
         Arcade's Tests.targets blanks the name with no guard on its current value, so setting
         TestRunnerName while UsingToolXUnit=false is still false would not have worked either.

         VSTest looks like the right name here because these are MSTest/VSTest tests, but there is
         no tools\VSTest\ directory - VSTest.targets sits at the tools root and is *imported by*
         the runner targets. tools\MSTest\MSTest.targets imports ..\VSTest.targets when
         EnableMSTestRunner is false, and that import is what defines RunTests. So MSTest is the
         name that works, and it is also what rel/17.14 sets. -->
    <TestRunnerName>MSTest</TestRunnerName>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>

